                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I haven't played this game very much but I really don't need more than 5 minutes to determine that this game is really not for me.\n\nFirst off, you're probably wondering what the hell Tengutana is.  You read the description and it sounds interesting. Well it's not really that interesting.  You basically start out in the level with this floating Tengu head (a tengu is a type of long-nosed Japanese demon known for trickery).  In front of you is a box with a glowing ball on it.  next to the glowing box on the ground is a \"put soul here\" marker... or maybe it says spirit...I forget now.  Either way, the glowing ball is the soul/spirit and you have to cut the box with your sword in such a way that the ball falls directly into the pad with the least number of cuts possible.\n\nIf this still sounds interesting, you're weird.  I mean, I'm all for puzzle games, and physics based puzzle games can be fun, but the problem is that how well you can solve the puzzle is tied directly to how good the physics engine is and how accurately you can manipulate things in that environment.  \n\nSo how is the physics engine?  Not terribly good.  Swinging swords feels hollow/empty with zero feedback to make you feel like you're swinging anything other than your arms around.  Cutting the boxes similarly doesn't feel like you're doing anything.  Either you hit the box and nothing happens, or you barely seem to touch the box and a slice appears going all the way through the box at whatever angle the blade happened to be pointing.\n\nIt's hit or miss... I guess I never miss huh?  (^^)\n\nSeriously though, that's most of the game right there.  They also add some stone blocks that you can't obviously chop through so for these you use your sword like a bat to nudge/push the stone out of the way.  Again, your success in getting the orb to drop where it's supposed to will feel more like luck and less like you solved a puzzle.  \n\nThere's also some shuriken throwing but so far as I can tell, all it's used for is as a way to change your character's size in relation to everything else.  Throw a shuriken at the tengu head and you become a giant.  Throw it again and you're back to normal size.  \n\nAnyway, the puzzles were lame, the game wasn't fun, and I wouldn't recommend you buy it even if you got it for a dime...It's not the worst VR game on steam, but it's hardly all that great either.  I think this is one of those games I got in a bundle with 10 other games for $3.",
